// bowl.mat — VM-091 (unified-engine migration Task 2). The camera bowl's
// material: per-fragment sampling (Decision resolution 2, 2026-09-11) of up
// to kMaxBowlCameras (6) camera textures, matching reproject.cu's bowl_kernel
// per-camera loop (right/down/fwd/t projection, plumb_bob distortion, border
// feather x alignment^2 weight) as closely as a rasterizer material can.
//
// UNLIT: the bowl's color IS the camera pixels, same rationale as
// point_cloud.mat/trajectory_carpet.mat -- PBR shading would add a
// light-direction term nobody asked for.
//
// The interpolant budget (MATERIAL_VARIABLES_COUNT = 4, Decision 3) holds
// TWO custom vec4s here, well inside budget:
//   CUSTOM0 (via `requires: [ color ]`, vertex passes material.color
//     through) = {coverage_a, index_a, coverage_b, index_b} -- the per-vertex
//     bake's output (bowl_mesh.cpp). index_a/index_b are LITERALLY IDENTICAL
//     across a triangle's three vertices by construction (Decision 3's
//     per-triangle-uniform-index rule), so linearly interpolating them
//     reproduces the same integer everywhere in the triangle -- safe to
//     round-trip through int(x + 0.5) in the fragment shader.
//   CUSTOM2 (via `requires: [ custom0 ]`, a real vertex attribute -- NOT
//     packed through uv0/uv1, which Filament's generated vertex shader
//     V-flips: fine for an actual texture UV, silently wrong for a position
//     payload) = the vertex's RIG-FRAME position (bowl_mesh.cpp bakes/
//     generates positions in rig frame -- Decision 3's frame convention).
//     This is NOT the same as Filament's own getWorldPosition(): the bowl
//     entity's TransformManager transform is the map<-rig ego pose
//     (renderer.cpp's update_bowl, matching update_ego_transform), so
//     getWorldPosition() would be MAP frame -- wrong frame for camera
//     extrinsics, which are rig-frame (scene.h). Carrying rig-frame position
//     as its own interpolant is what makes "per-fragment sampling from the
//     rasterized [rig-frame] position" (Decision resolution 2) mean the
//     position is interpolated PER FRAGMENT across the triangle (much finer
//     than the per-vertex bake this decision replaced), not merely read once
//     per vertex. rigPos rides `filament::VertexAttribute::CUSTOM0` (FLOAT3,
//     bound in bowl.cpp's make_bowl_vertex_buffer), requested here via
//     `requires: [ custom0 ]` and read with `getCustom0()` in
//     materialVertex -- Filament passes CUSTOM attributes through with no
//     per-component arithmetic. It is then carried across the rasterizer on
//     its own interpolant, `custom2` (custom1 is not declared -- nothing
//     uses it), exactly like weight/index use custom0.
//
// PARAMETER BUDGET, empirically discovered, not assumed: this matc build
// (1.56.5) aborts with "Too many parameters" past a per-material uniform
// float-count ceiling well below what MAX_SAMPLER_COUNT/interpolant-budget
// math alone would predict (verified experimentally: a material with 6
// cameras' full right/down/fwd/t + K + dist(4) + widthHeightK3(4) [24
// floats/camera, 148 total] aborts; 5 cameras at that footprint [124 total]
// does not). This material is therefore deliberately trimmed to 18
// floats/camera (108 total + 6 shared = 114):
//   - Per-camera parameters are 6 SEPARATELY NAMED uniforms each (camRightN/
//     camFwdN/camTN/camKN/camDistN/camK3_N/camTexN), not a `size:`-declared
//     array: this matc build does not correctly support array-typed
//     material parameters at all (verified separately -- a
//     `{ type: float4, name: arr, size: 6 }` parameter compiles, but
//     `materialParams.arr[i]` type-checks as a scalar float / single-vec4
//     access, not an array index, for both constant and dynamic indices).
//     This also sidesteps a real hazard the plan names for a SAMPLER
//     array: selecting a sampler by a fragment-computed index is
//     non-dynamically-uniform sampler indexing (illegal/UB in GLSL) -- the
//     fragment shader below instead unrolls a static (compile-time-
//     constant) `addCam()` call per camera, each gated by a runtime
//     equality check (`idxA == N`) against the baked index rather than by
//     indexing a sampler array, so no dynamic sampler indexing ever occurs
//     (sampler2D is a legal GLSL function parameter -- passing the already-
//     bound texture into `addCam()` is not dynamic indexing either).
//   - NO per-camera `down` uniform: reconstructed in-shader as
//     cross(fwd, right) -- exact because camera_ingest.cpp orthonormalizes
//     every camera's R (Gram-Schmidt on ingest, WARNing if the correction is
//     more than cosmetic) before it reaches BowlConfig, so right/fwd are
//     always the first and third columns of a true rotation matrix.
//   - NO per-camera width/height uniform: read via textureSize() on the
//     already-bound camera texture, which is exactly the camera's real
//     pixel dimensions (BowlConfig's cam_width/cam_height are what sized
//     the texture in the first place, camera_textures.cpp).
//   - NO per-camera mat4 ego-motion-delta uniform: composing it into the
//     EFFECTIVE right/fwd/t above on the CPU (bowl.cpp's update_bowl, once
//     per render_frame call -- a 3x3 transpose-multiply per camera, no
//     matrix inverse needed since an ego-motion delta's rotation is
//     orthonormal) is both well inside this budget and cheaper than a
//     GPU-side mat4 multiply per fragment would have been.
//
// Border feather is computed PER-FRAGMENT, from the `featherMargin`
// parameter (bowl.cpp pushes BowlConfig::feather_margin), ported verbatim
// from blend.cuh's border_feather/smoothstep01 at the fragment's own xp/yp
// -- a vertex-baked feather would hard-reject at `xp > width-1` carrying the
// full interpolated vertex weight right up to that line, stepping instead of
// the smoothstep falloff reproject.cu produces. coverage_a/coverage_b
// (bowl_mesh.cpp) carry only the alignment^2 term.
//
// Texture UV is pixel-CENTER sampled (`(xp,yp) + 0.5`), not pixel-CORNER
// (`xp/width`): matches GL's own texel center convention and reproject.cu's
// `bilinear()`, which samples at xp exactly. `bowl_projection.cpp`'s
// `ProjectToCameraUv` keeps its own pixel-CORNER convention (stated
// explicitly in its own header) -- this +0.5 is applied here, shader-side,
// only, converting xp/yp (which bowl_mesh.cpp already derives as
// `u*width`/`v*height`) back to a pixel-center-sampled UV.
//
// skyColor mirrors BowlConfig's own sky_color field -- bowl.cpp pushes it
// once per set_bowl_config() call.
//
// exposureCompensation is a material PARAMETER (BowlConfig::
// exposure_compensation, default 1.5, bowl.cpp pushes it), not a hardcoded
// shader constant: a style knob alongside sky_color/feather_margin, per the
// STANDING "every rendered element ships style tokens" directive. It
// intentionally does NOT multiply skyColor (see the fragment code below) --
// sky_color is authored already in display/post-tonemap space (what a user
// picks in a color swatch), not in the same pre-tonemap space sampled camera
// pixels live in, so multiplying it by exposureCompensation would make any
// sky_color above ~0.1 clip to white for no reason a user configuring
// sky_color would expect.
//
// This material contributes at most 2 cameras per fragment (CUSTOM0/CUSTOM2
// are both spoken for: weight/index and rigPos), and the top-2 pair is
// chosen PER TRIANGLE (bowl_mesh.cpp), so across a triple-camera-overlap
// band adjacent triangles can switch from blending (A,B) to (A,C) at equal
// summed weight -- a mesh-aligned faceted seam reproject.cu's all-camera
// blend does not have. Recorded as a NAMED PARITY EXCEPTION (Task 4 Step 1's
// checklist, migration plan doc) rather than fixed here --
// test_bowl.cpp's ThreeCameraOverlapStillPicksAConsistentPairPerTriangle
// documents the current (2-camera) behavior at a real triple-overlap
// triangle so a future fix has a test to change deliberately, not silently.
//
// OPAQUE (no `blending:` line -- Filament's default): the STANDING
// opaque-while-fresh convention (ribbon_emissive.mat's header) applies here
// too -- the bowl is either fully covered (alpha 1, some camera contributed)
// or shows sky_color (also alpha 1); it is never a blended/translucent
// surface, so it must never sit in Filament's blended queue.
material {
    name : bowl,
    shadingModel : unlit,
    parameters : [
        { type : sampler2d, name : camTex0 },
        { type : sampler2d, name : camTex1 },
        { type : sampler2d, name : camTex2 },
        { type : sampler2d, name : camTex3 },
        { type : sampler2d, name : camTex4 },
        { type : sampler2d, name : camTex5 },
        { type : float3, name : camRight0 },
        { type : float3, name : camFwd0 },
        { type : float3, name : camT0 },
        { type : float4, name : camK0 },
        { type : float4, name : camDist0 },
        { type : float, name : camK3_0 },
        { type : float3, name : camRight1 },
        { type : float3, name : camFwd1 },
        { type : float3, name : camT1 },
        { type : float4, name : camK1 },
        { type : float4, name : camDist1 },
        { type : float, name : camK3_1 },
        { type : float3, name : camRight2 },
        { type : float3, name : camFwd2 },
        { type : float3, name : camT2 },
        { type : float4, name : camK2 },
        { type : float4, name : camDist2 },
        { type : float, name : camK3_2 },
        { type : float3, name : camRight3 },
        { type : float3, name : camFwd3 },
        { type : float3, name : camT3 },
        { type : float4, name : camK3 },
        { type : float4, name : camDist3 },
        { type : float, name : camK3_3 },
        { type : float3, name : camRight4 },
        { type : float3, name : camFwd4 },
        { type : float3, name : camT4 },
        { type : float4, name : camK4 },
        { type : float4, name : camDist4 },
        { type : float, name : camK3_4 },
        { type : float3, name : camRight5 },
        { type : float3, name : camFwd5 },
        { type : float3, name : camT5 },
        { type : float4, name : camK5 },
        { type : float4, name : camDist5 },
        { type : float, name : camK3_5 },
        { type : float3, name : skyColor },
        { type : float, name : featherMargin },
        { type : float, name : exposureCompensation }
    ],
    requires : [ color, custom0 ],
    variables : [ custom0, custom2 ]
}
vertex {
    void materialVertex(inout MaterialVertexInputs material) {
        material.custom0 = material.color;
        material.custom2 = vec4(getCustom0().xyz, 0.0);
    }
}
fragment {
    // One camera's contribution to colorSum/wsum -- right/fwd/t are the
    // EFFECTIVE (ego-motion-delta-composed) extrinsics bowl.cpp uploads
    // each render_frame call; `down` is reconstructed as cross(fwd,right)
    // rather than uploaded (see this material's header for the
    // parameter-budget reason and the orthonormality guarantee). width/
    // height come from textureSize(tex), not a uniform. `coverage` is
    // this camera's already-selected per-triangle baked weight (0 if this
    // camera isn't one of the triangle's top-2, computed by the caller).
    // No dynamic sampler indexing: `tex` is passed in already bound to a
    // statically-named sampler at each of the 6 call sites below.
    void addCam(sampler2D tex, vec3 right, vec3 fwd, vec3 t, vec4 K, vec4 dist, float k3,
                float coverage, vec3 rigPos, inout vec3 colorSum, inout float wsum) {
        if (coverage <= 0.0) return;
        // Exact, not approximate: camera_ingest.cpp orthonormalizes every
        // camera's R on ingest, so right/fwd are always true rotation columns.
        vec3 down = cross(fwd, right);
        vec3 rel = rigPos - t;
        float zc = dot(fwd, rel);
        if (zc <= 1e-9) return;
        float xn = dot(right, rel) / zc;
        float yn = dot(down, rel) / zc;
        float k1 = dist.x;
        float k2 = dist.y;
        float p1 = dist.z;
        float p2 = dist.w;
        bool distorted = (k1 != 0.0 || k2 != 0.0 || p1 != 0.0 || p2 != 0.0 || k3 != 0.0);
        float r2 = xn * xn + yn * yn;
        if (distorted) {
            if (r2 > 3.0) return;
            float radial = 1.0 + r2 * (k1 + r2 * (k2 + r2 * k3));
            float xd = xn * radial + 2.0 * p1 * xn * yn + p2 * (r2 + 2.0 * xn * xn);
            float yd = yn * radial + p1 * (r2 + 2.0 * yn * yn) + 2.0 * p2 * xn * yn;
            xn = xd;
            yn = yd;
        }
        float fx = K.x;
        float fy = K.y;
        float cx = K.z;
        float cy = K.w;
        ivec2 dims = textureSize(tex, 0);
        float width = float(dims.x);
        float height = float(dims.y);
        float xp = fx * xn + cx;
        float yp = fy * yn + cy;
        if (xp < 0.0 || xp > width - 1.0 || yp < 0.0 || yp > height - 1.0) return;
        float d = min(min(xp, (width - 1.0) - xp), min(yp, (height - 1.0) - yp));
        float feather;
        if (materialParams.featherMargin <= 0.0) {
            feather = step(0.0, d);
        } else {
            float ratio = clamp(d / materialParams.featherMargin, 0.0, 1.0);
            feather = ratio * ratio * (3.0 - 2.0 * ratio);
        }
        float wgt = coverage * feather;
        if (wgt <= 0.0) return;
        vec2 uv = (vec2(xp, yp) + 0.5) / vec2(width, height);
        colorSum += wgt * texture(tex, uv).rgb;
        wsum += wgt;
    }

    void material(inout MaterialInputs material) {
        prepareMaterial(material);
        vec4 c0 = variable_custom0;
        vec3 rigPos = variable_custom2.xyz;
        float coverageA = c0.x;
        int idxA = int(c0.y + 0.5);
        float coverageB = c0.z;
        int idxB = int(c0.w + 0.5);

        vec3 colorSum = vec3(0.0);
        float wsum = 0.0;

        addCam(materialParams_camTex0, materialParams.camRight0, materialParams.camFwd0,
               materialParams.camT0, materialParams.camK0, materialParams.camDist0,
               materialParams.camK3_0,
               (idxA == 0) ? coverageA : ((idxB == 0) ? coverageB : 0.0), rigPos, colorSum, wsum);
        addCam(materialParams_camTex1, materialParams.camRight1, materialParams.camFwd1,
               materialParams.camT1, materialParams.camK1, materialParams.camDist1,
               materialParams.camK3_1,
               (idxA == 1) ? coverageA : ((idxB == 1) ? coverageB : 0.0), rigPos, colorSum, wsum);
        addCam(materialParams_camTex2, materialParams.camRight2, materialParams.camFwd2,
               materialParams.camT2, materialParams.camK2, materialParams.camDist2,
               materialParams.camK3_2,
               (idxA == 2) ? coverageA : ((idxB == 2) ? coverageB : 0.0), rigPos, colorSum, wsum);
        addCam(materialParams_camTex3, materialParams.camRight3, materialParams.camFwd3,
               materialParams.camT3, materialParams.camK3, materialParams.camDist3,
               materialParams.camK3_3,
               (idxA == 3) ? coverageA : ((idxB == 3) ? coverageB : 0.0), rigPos, colorSum, wsum);
        addCam(materialParams_camTex4, materialParams.camRight4, materialParams.camFwd4,
               materialParams.camT4, materialParams.camK4, materialParams.camDist4,
               materialParams.camK3_4,
               (idxA == 4) ? coverageA : ((idxB == 4) ? coverageB : 0.0), rigPos, colorSum, wsum);
        addCam(materialParams_camTex5, materialParams.camRight5, materialParams.camFwd5,
               materialParams.camT5, materialParams.camK5, materialParams.camDist5,
               materialParams.camK3_5,
               (idxA == 5) ? coverageA : ((idxB == 5) ? coverageB : 0.0), rigPos, colorSum, wsum);

        // This renderer's fixed camera exposure (renderer.cpp's
        // setExposure(16, 1/500, 100), tuned against SUN-lit clay surfaces)
        // crushes an UNLIT material's raw baseColor -- an unlit (1,1,1)
        // renders at only ~15% output brightness (measured empirically
        // against this exact material, no lighting to compensate the way
        // clay.mat's albedo relies on the sun). The CUDA reference
        // (reproject.cu) writes camera RGB straight to its output buffer
        // with NO exposure/tonemap step at all, so passing sampled camera
        // pixels through unscaled would make the bowl read as a dim,
        // washed-out image compared to the CUDA node's own output -- a real
        // fidelity regression, not just a test-sentinel artifact. 1.5x
        // (retuned from an initial 10.0x guess at Task 2 Step 7's golden
        // capture, real six-camera bag stack_v2_full_sensors_2026-09-09 --
        // 10.0x blew the bowl out to near-white against this bag's real
        // daytime CARLA exposure; matc/this build's tonemap curve is
        // nonlinear/saturating, so this is a working compensation matched
        // against the CUDA reference's own brightness, not a precise
        // inverse) brings a well-lit camera pixel back into the CUDA node's
        // own display range. skyColor is deliberately NOT multiplied
        // by it (see this material's header) -- sky_color is authored in
        // display space already, not the pre-tonemap camera-pixel space
        // exposureCompensation corrects for.
        if (wsum > 0.0) {
            material.baseColor.rgb = (colorSum / wsum) * materialParams.exposureCompensation;
        } else {
            material.baseColor.rgb = materialParams.skyColor;
        }
        material.baseColor.a = 1.0;
    }
}
